.TH UNCIX 1 "CIX 0.1.0" "CIX" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uncix \- restore, test, or inspect a CIX archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uncix
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-t | \-l ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RI [ archive ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uncix
restores a CIX archive to its original byte stream. With a named archive, the
default output name removes a final
.B .cix
suffix when present. Existing outputs are not replaced unless
.B \-f
is supplied. Omit ARCHIVE or use
.B \-
to read an archive from standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Write restored bytes to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-o " " \fIFILE\fR
Write restored bytes to FILE.
.TP
.B \-f
Replace an existing output file.
.TP
.B \-k
Accepted for compatibility. Input archives are retained by default.
.TP
.B \-t
Test archive integrity without writing restored bytes.
.TP
.B \-l
List archive information on standard error while restoring.
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Show command usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the CIX version and supported container identifiers.
.SH RESOURCE LIMITS
The decoder starts with a 6 GiB accounted working-memory admission limit unless an explicit limit is supplied. This
is not an aggregate-process RSS guarantee; use ordinary operating-system process limits when restoring untrusted archives. Compression
options, including effort, route, format, block-size, profile, and parallelism,
are rejected while decoding.
.SH SECURITY
Archives from untrusted sources are untrusted input. Use
.B uncix -t
when an integrity check is needed without writing restored bytes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cix (1),
.BR cixcat (1)
